  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This Figshare dataset presents an assembled transcriptome and predicted nonredundant proteome for the choanoflagellate <i>Choanoeca flexa</i>. The record shows one documented downstream reuse and two scite mentions, indicating early but tangible uptake of the dataset in later scholarly work.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published in 2019 as an openly accessible dataset by Thibaut Brunet, this resource contributes transcriptomic and proteomic sequence data for <i>Choanoeca flexa</i>, a relevant organism for comparative biology and gene-expression studies. The concept profile centers on transcriptome and proteome analysis, with additional alignment to computational biology, bioinformatics, genetics, and gene expression, matching the dataset\u2019s role as a reusable molecular resource rather than a traditional narrative article.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Evidence of impact is modest but clear. OpenAlex currently records no citations to the dataset, and the available attention indicators are quiet, with no event-data mentions and no recorded Datacite views or downloads in the digest. That said, the record does show one downstream reuse, captured as a biomedical article mention in Europe PMC, which is the strongest sign that the dataset has been taken up in subsequent research.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The stewardship signals are consistent with reuse-oriented dissemination: the dataset is deposited in Figshare, a repository with open database access and DOI-based persistence, and OpenAIRE identifies two open instances of the dataset record. This supports findability and access, which are especially important for sequence resources intended for later computational analysis.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Scite also reports two total mentions, both classified as mentioning rather than supporting or contradicting, suggesting that the dataset is being referenced in the literature without yet accumulating a large citation footprint. Overall, the evidence points to a small but concrete research afterlife: the dataset is publicly available, technically well positioned for reuse, and already appears in later biomedical scholarship, even though broader citation and attention signals remain limited.</p>",
